00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.140
business under their desk.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:00.830
Right?

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.950
I mean, it was kind of the wild
wild west, but certainly not

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.130
the way to build a resilient,
a reliable, a fault tolerant

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.090
business.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.980
And we then moved to
managed data centers.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.100
We then moved to colos.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.380
We got more efficiency.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.680
We moved to VMs where you'd pack
more onto the machine itself.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.007
And then public cloud was
a step function change,

00:03:20.007 --> 00:03:22.340
making it much, much easier
to provision infrastructure,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.380
much, much easier
to build systems.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.270
And then even on
top of public cloud,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.970
we've added a lot of services
to make things even easier

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.490
on you as a developer,
things like App Engine, Big

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.310
Query, Cloud Storage, Compute
Engine, Data Store, Firebase,

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.340
Data Flow, and many
other technologies.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.130
But there's still a
lot of challenges.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.750
I mean, it's not it's not a
completely solved problem.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:45.820
There's complexity.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.154
Even a modest internet
business these days--

00:03:48.154 --> 00:03:49.570
let's say you're
doing e-commerce.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.255
A small e-commerce site, you've
probably got a users database,

00:03:52.255 --> 00:03:54.750
a products database, a
transactions database.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.500
You're getting a number
of different APIs.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.960
It's necessarily complex.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.340
You've got inefficient
infrastructure, many of you,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.060
in that, if you're running on
traditional infrastructure--

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.630
let's say you have nothing
more than a diurnal pattern

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:07.700
to your usage.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.445
Let's say you're in a single
geography, single continent.

00:04:11.445 --> 00:04:13.570
When your users are asleep
and not actually hitting

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.850
your service, you're
still running servers.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.490
You're inefficient, and then
these systems, at times,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.680
can be very difficult to extend.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.890
It's the corollary of
the complexity point

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.880
that the more
complex the system,

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.590
every new feature
you want to add

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:30.457
ends up being surgery
that you do that system.

00:04:30.457 --> 00:04:32.040
And so you know it's
called Doing More

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.350
With Less, Serverless
and Events,

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.725
so know I'm going to say that
Serveless plus Events helps

00:04:35.725 --> 00:04:36.970
you solve these problems.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.130
So how does it do it?

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:39.620
So one, it's very
simple, and I'm

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.280
going to talk more about
each one of these points.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.150
It's just code.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.230
You give us your code.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.520
We do everything else.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.630
It's fully managed.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.030
Again, we run your code.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.080
We take care of patching,
and auto scaling,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.250
and everything else.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.040
You just give us your
actual business logic,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.334
and we do everything else.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:56.625
And we automatically make
connections between services,

00:04:56.625 --> 00:04:59.010
so you don't need to think
about APIs, and handshakes,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.420
and all that.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.050
It's efficient in that
you pay for what you use.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:06.150
Again, in that diurnal
example, with Serverless,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.970
when you're users are
asleep, you're not paying.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.590
It's event driven.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.400
So again, we only spin up
compute when there's actually

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.584
an event to process.

00:05:15.584 --> 00:05:17.000
And then finally,
it's extensible,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.390
so the very nature of Serverless
encourages us to write software

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.830
as microservices.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.710
And so adding a new feature,
adding new functionality,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.780
is as simple as just
adding a new microservice

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.730
to your overall application.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.230
And so now let's
talk really briefly

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:35.500
about Google's Serverless
event-driven framework

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.320
Cloud Functions.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.290
And yes, I'm calling
it a framework in that,

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.600
very much like the web
and app frameworks,

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.130
Cloud Functions is
a framework that

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.700
sits on top of Google's
infrastructure, that lets you

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.270
very simply access a number
of services and just focus

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.192
on writing your code.

00:05:51.192 --> 00:05:52.150
And so it's Serverless.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.110
What does that mean?

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:54.340
No server management.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.170
You don't even need to
think about servers.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:57.310
Simple deployment model.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:58.390
Just give us your code.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.200
I can't say that enough times.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.590
We auto-scale it up and
down, and you pay only

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.210
for what you use.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.590
It's even driven.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:08.730
Seems pretty simple, but
let me just go through it.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.460
An event is admitted.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.190
If your function is
configured to listen

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.610
to that particular event,
we then spin it up.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:16.980
Your code executes.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.460
And so what does this look like?

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.950
This looks like just focusing
on your business logic.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.780
We had all those
things that needed

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.030
to happen to actually
get your code in a place

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.860
where it could spin
up and run and respond

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.060
to things on that
very first slide,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.720
or I guess that second slide.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.906
But this is where your focused,
just your business logic now.

00:06:34.906 --> 00:06:37.570
And Cloud Function supports
events for more than 20 Google

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:38.630
services.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.540
So many of the most
popular Google services

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.430
are already integrated
with Cloud Functions,

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.554
and we're adding
more all the time.

00:06:44.554 --> 00:06:45.970
And this is really
exciting for us

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.210
because each new
source that we add

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.550
ends up unlocking a whole
new set of use cases.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.841
And you can imagine that the
things you can build with,

00:06:53.841 --> 00:06:55.870
let's say, a Cloud
build integration,

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.835
or a Stackdriver
Logging integration

00:06:57.835 --> 00:06:59.710
are quite different from
the things you might

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:01.780
build with a Gmail integration.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.240
And so every time we open
up one of these new sources,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:07.630
developers just amaze us and wow
us with the things they build.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.010
Frankly, you guys are much
more creative than we are.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:11.640
And so a recap.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.100
Why should you do it
this way, this Serverless

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.310
and even driven way?

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.970
Again, it's simple,
it's efficient,

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.600
and it's extensible,
easily accessible.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.044
So don't just take
my word for it.

00:07:22.044 --> 00:07:23.210
That's enough of the theory.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:24.543
Let's look at an actual example.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.080
So in the traditional
development model,

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.815
I'm going to go to a situation
or scenario that many of you

00:07:31.815 --> 00:07:32.649
have probably faced.

00:07:32.649 --> 00:07:34.689
If you've done anything
consumer facing at least,

00:07:34.689 --> 00:07:36.070
you've probably
faced a situation

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.680
where you want to
allow people to create

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.680
profiles or some
sort of artifact

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.369
that they exist in your system.

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:45.160
And so if you've got
profiles, you probably

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.490
want to have a photo
associated with that profile.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.600
So the user, let's say,
want's to upload a photo,

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.690
and then you want to
crop it and store it down

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.610
for the profile's page.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.650
How would you do that in
the traditional model?

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.470
Well, you probably
stand up a server

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:02.050
and use a single server
rout to do everything.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:03.951
And so the user would
upload the photo,

00:08:03.951 --> 00:08:05.950
the route would crop the
image and store it down

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.360
into the profile's directory.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.140
That's not too bad.

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:12.410
But now I want to extend that.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.784
I want to resize
that same image,

00:08:13.784 --> 00:08:15.640
and I want to use it
for my user gallery.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:16.300
Right?

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.762
I've got a number of
images, let's say.

00:08:18.762 --> 00:08:20.470
I want to be able
actually have a gallery

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.505
and see all the
users that are there.

00:08:22.505 --> 00:08:23.380
How would we do that?

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.120
Well, we just add more
logic to our server router.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.610
So now we upload the photo.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:28.660
We crop the image.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:30.560
We store it into
storage and profiles.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.262
We now resize the photo.

00:08:32.262 --> 00:08:33.720
We stored into the
gallery section.

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:39.960
That was a fair amount of
work, but at least we're done.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.700
But now, let's say I want
to index those profile

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.220
images for searches.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.810
Let's say some meta data comes
in, and it's the user's name.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.600
You should do a search
via name for some profile

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.540
that's on the system.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.620
So what do I do?

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:56.380
Well, let's just add even more
logic to that server route.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.680
So upload the image, crop
it, stored in profiles,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.590
resize the image,
store it in a gallery,

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.340
and now take that
metadata-- in this case,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:06.060
I've shown the first name,
last name, and the image URL--

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.899
and let's write
that into search.

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:11.190
Is anyone starting to feel
a little bit like this woman

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:12.760
at the edge of this cliff?

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:13.510
Very cool picture.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:14.320
I don't know how they took it.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.972
It looks like she's
going to fall.

00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:18.200
But what's wrong
with this model?

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:18.700
Right?

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.116
What are some of
the shortcomings.

00:09:20.116 --> 00:09:21.640
Well, it's incredibly complex.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.520
There's a lot going on
inside that single route.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.770
It's hard to understand
because so much is going on.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.270
It's hard to debug.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:29.862
The infrastructure
is inefficient.

00:09:29.862 --> 00:09:32.320
We have a server in the middle
to do nothing but just write

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.340
things to storage.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.240
It doesn't need to be there.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.140
We have servers running
whether there's traffic or not.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.790
And then it's
difficult to extend.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.759
Every change requires
surgery to route

00:09:41.759 --> 00:09:43.300
because we keep
adding more and more,

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:45.716
and I have even talked about
some of the hairy distributed

00:09:45.716 --> 00:09:47.980
systems problems
associated with failures.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.680
What happens?

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.138
You know, how do
we handle retries?

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.570
And so is there
another way to do this?

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.024
Again, the title is
Serverless+Events.

00:09:58.024 --> 00:09:59.940
You know I'm going to
go to Serverless Events.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.920
So yes, there's another
way, but let's look

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:07.736
at how the Event-driven model
helps to solve this problem.

00:10:07.736 --> 00:10:09.610
So how would we do this
with Cloud Functions,

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.410
Google's event-driven,
Serverless framework?

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:16.610
Well, let's say a
user would then upload

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.130
the file direct to storage.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:19.190
WE don't need a server.

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:21.920
There are no servers in
this picture whatsoever.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.790
That would then cause
an event to fire.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.370
This is called object.finalize.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.850
The name is unimportant, but
it's basically a create event.

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.850
That then causes a function to
trigger, which crops the image

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.250
and stores it down
into profiles.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:35.390
No servers on this page.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.910
Nothing for you as a
developer to operate,

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:41.160
less code, less
infrastructure, less work.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.532
With Cloud Functions, we
really can do more with less.

00:10:46.532 --> 00:10:48.615
And so let's come back to
some of those advantages

00:10:48.615 --> 00:10:51.090
that I talked about on
that slide with the more

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.870
simple, efficient, extensible.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.390
Let's start with simple.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.000
So there's less
boilerplate code to write.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.840
And then even in the
very, very lightweight web

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.460
serving framework, like
Flask here in Python,

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.080
there's a lot of boilerplate.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:06.324
I've got to import Flask.

00:11:06.324 --> 00:11:07.990
I've got to decorate
my route to tell it

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.340
where to send the request.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:11.970
I've got write a main function
to actually run the app.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.110
There's just a lot
here that has nothing

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.610
to do with the business logic.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:19.100
The business logic is really
just in two lines of code here.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.100
And for those of you who
actually know something

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.810
about deployment, if you're not
just writing the back end code,

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:26.460
but potentially
deploying it as well,

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.590
you know I've skipped
an entire step, which

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.950
is having to configure NGINX.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.670
How do I configuring my proxy?

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:37.510
Who here believes that the
way they add superior value

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.720
to their users is by
configuring NGINX better

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:41.779
than the competition?

00:11:41.779 --> 00:11:42.820
It's somewhat rhetorical.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:44.590
Please, no one raise their hand.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:45.864
But it's true, right?

00:11:45.864 --> 00:11:47.530
It's something we all
have to deal with,

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:49.181
and it adds almost
no user value.

00:11:49.181 --> 00:11:51.430
And to be clear, so you don't
feel like I'm beating up

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.055
on NGINX, I think it's great.

00:11:53.055 --> 00:11:53.680
It's wonderful.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.390
In fact, we, here
at Google, package

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.870
NGINX into many of our
images for our run times.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:00.905
But again, it's something
we should worry about,

00:12:00.905 --> 00:12:03.280
not something that you as a
developer should worry about.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.200
You should just worry
about your code.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.150
And let's look at
what happens when

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.860
we start doing this in
the Serverless Events way.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.480
It's just the business logic,
just those two lines of code.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:14.740
Google is your framework.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:18.590
Google is your proxy.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:21.932
It's also simple in the sense
that it's single purpose.

00:12:21.932 --> 00:12:23.390
So we go from
something like this--

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.760
and I know I've kind of loaded
this slide by calling it

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.680
the monolith handler--

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.480
but we go from a
handler that's doing

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:30.600
a lot of different things.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:31.560
It's cropping an image.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:32.690
It's saving it to storage.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.940
It's resizing the image,
saving it to storage.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:38.996
It's indexing the metadata.

00:12:38.996 --> 00:12:41.370
And again, I didn't even talk
about retries and failures.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.270
I showed a pretty simple case.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:47.820
We move from that to this, where
everything is single purpose.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.915
We're still doing all
same steps, admittedly.

00:12:49.915 --> 00:12:51.040
We still have to resize it.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:51.700
You have to crop it.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:52.910
You have to index things.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:54.560
But everything is
single purpose,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.300
and it's much easier
to understand,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.010
much easier to debug.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.500
And oh, you know those
distributed systems problems

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.260
associated with
failures and retries,

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.430
you know who handles that?

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:05.246
Google.

00:13:05.246 --> 00:13:06.620
We retry your code
when it fails.

00:13:10.575 --> 00:13:11.950
And it's also
simple in the sense

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.180
that we make automatic
connections between services.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.630
And so this is a point
that at times can get lost.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:20.350
But if I want to talk from
service A to service B--

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:23.500
let's say it's Cloud Storage
talking to a Cloud Function--

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:24.520
I don't have to pull.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.852
I don't have to check to
say, did something change?

00:13:26.852 --> 00:13:27.560
Is something new?

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:28.268
Is something new?

00:13:28.268 --> 00:13:29.050
Is something new?

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.450
When most of the time,
the answer is, no.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:35.180
The event is delivered directly
to my Serverless function.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.120
There are no APIs to contact.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:39.940
I don't need to figure out
how to go talk to the service

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:41.560
to go get a piece of data.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:45.160
I don't need to know how to go
talk to a service to configure

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.350
it to talk to my function.

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:49.480
All the data that
I need is delivered

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:54.560
to my event, delivered directly
to my function in most cases.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:58.300
And then finally, we broker
those connections automatically

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.440
in the sense that it literally
takes about five seconds

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:05.110
in the UI, maybe two seconds
in the command line, to say,

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:09.640
wire function x up to
Cloud storage bucket B,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:11.830
making it very,
very simple for you

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.940
to build sophisticated
and powerful systems.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.021
It's also very efficient.

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:17.520
I won't belabor
this point because I

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.470
think you've heard it
many times, but if you

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.060
have an input that looks
like this, something that's

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:21.660
kind of spiky.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.060
Let's say you're
a startup company,

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.490
and you have very few
users adding profiles

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:28.590
in the very beginning of
your company's evolution.

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.260
Or maybe you're very
mature, and people already

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:32.677
have the profiles
on their system.

00:14:32.677 --> 00:14:34.468
And let's pretend that
this is not Facebook

00:14:34.468 --> 00:14:36.600
where people change their
profile every single day,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.431
but a normal website where you
might change it once a year.

00:14:40.431 --> 00:14:42.180
Your input could very
well look like this.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.120
With Cloud Functions, your
bill would track the input.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:47.660
With the traditional
server dominated model,

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:50.970
when that server's up,
you're paying for it.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.370
And then it's also
very extensible.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.190
So let's go back to
that original example.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:59.260
We were already bursting at
the seams with complexity

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:00.829
when we just had three steps.

00:15:00.829 --> 00:15:02.370
Now let's say I want
to add a fourth.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.536
Let's say I want to actually
extract information out

00:15:04.536 --> 00:15:05.140
of the image.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.110
I don't want to just take the
metadata that you've provided.

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:08.901
I want to see what's
going on in the image.

00:15:08.901 --> 00:15:11.010
Maybe I want to make
it a visual search,

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.720
and then I want to
index those attributes

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.280
for even better searches.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:15.580
So how could I do this?

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:19.230
Well, I could just add
more logic to the server.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.470
I could upload the photo, crop
it and save it, resize and save

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.350
it, index the metadata, run
it through the Vision API,

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:30.800
and analyze the information
and index those tags.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.260
But that's a lot of work.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.490
That's a lot of surgery
that we have to go do.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:39.840
Whereas with the Serverless
model, the Event-driven model,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.080
let's say I saved
the original image,

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.550
and I'm triggering these
three function flows--

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.130
the crop and save, the resize
and save, the index metadata--

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.260
I've already got this.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.150
Adding a new piece
of functionality

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:55.370
is as simple as just adding
another action to the upload

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:56.650
event--

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:58.750
much, much simpler and
much more extensible,

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.490
no surgery required.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.020
And so a recap--

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:05.360
it's simple; it's just
code; it's fully managed;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:06.950
we automate all the connections.

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:07.710
It's efficient.

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.710
You pay for what you use,
and it's Event-driven.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.502
Then finally, it's
highly extensible,

00:16:11.502 --> 00:16:13.460
in that you're basically
writing microservices,

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:17.662
so extending it is as simple
as adding a new microservice.

00:16:17.662 --> 00:16:18.870
So don't take my word for it.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.620
Let's look at how some customers
are using Serverless+Events

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.062
to build very
sophisticated systems.

00:16:24.062 --> 00:16:26.020
So the first customer
we're going to talk about

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:28.990
is Smart Parking, and they're
building a realtime analytics

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.800
system.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.410
Little bit of information
on Smart Parking.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.650
So they've been
around for 15 years.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:40.470
They're in Australia,
New Zealand, and the UK.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.560
They've got Tens of thousands
of sensors in production,

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:47.060
and they are powered by
Google Cloud platform.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:49.060
Let's hear a little bit
more about what they do.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:50.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:50.262 --> 00:16:51.720
SPEAKER 1: The
number of cars using

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.240
urban roads is on the rise,
putting additional strain

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:57.580
