and sends realtime information

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:10.560
around guidance and
availability to display

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.070
such as our mobile app,
overhead occupancy indicators,

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:15.719
and guidance signage.

00:18:15.719 --> 00:18:19.947
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:19.947 --> 00:18:21.530
MORGAN HALLMON: So
I know a lot of you

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:24.560
came in from different locales,
but if your city looks anything

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.570
like San Francisco, I
know you're probably

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:29.150
asking one question
after seeing that video.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.160
When is this coming to my city?

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.200
Very, very interesting solution.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:34.890
They're doing some
wonderful things.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.306
So what are some
of the challenges

00:18:36.306 --> 00:18:39.249
associated with building a
realtime data analytics system?

00:18:39.249 --> 00:18:41.540
Well, one, you need up to
the minute, sometimes even up

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.620
to the second, freshness.

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:45.260
You could imagine that
having stale data, if you're

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.990
the operator of a parking
lot or parking garage,

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:49.330
can be a huge problem.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.770
If there's an event
going on, such as a ball

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:55.570
game or a concert, being stale
by even as much as 10 minutes

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:57.950
could show a very,
very different picture.

00:18:57.950 --> 00:19:01.190
Two, the data's coming from
a disparate set of sources.

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:03.510
So you saw in the video,
they've got in-ground sensors.

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:04.880
They've got overhead sensors.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:07.640
They've got plate readers.

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:10.820
They've got car counters,
many different sensors,

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:12.600
providing data into this system.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:14.590
And so as a result,
what do you have to do?

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:15.520
You've got to
normalize that data,

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.210
you've got to
transform that data,

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:20.180
and then finally you've got to
aggregate it, so a lot of data

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:24.560
manipulation that Smart
Parking has to do.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:25.910
And how are they doing it?

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:28.470
Well, with this
architecture here.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.070
So this is their Smart
Cloud architecture,

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:33.039
and you can see they
have a number of devices

00:19:33.039 --> 00:19:35.330
that are sending information
from the different parking

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:36.050
spots.

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.550
They've got their
Smart Spots, which

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:40.820
do a little bit of aggregation
of just the raw data,

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:42.860
and then the backbone
of this system

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:45.950
is really Google IoT Core.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:48.920
It's taking in all the
data and piping that

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:52.160
into GCP through
Pub/Sub, at which point

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.530
it then enters two
different data flows.

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:58.220
So one is the event flow,
where the raw data is simply

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.657
being piped in and saved
into persistent storage.

00:20:01.657 --> 00:20:03.740
And if you notice, this
whole thing is Serverless.

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:06.080
There's no infrastructure
to operate.

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:07.700
And then there's the data flow.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:09.029
And it's almost inap--

00:20:09.029 --> 00:20:11.320
I shouldn't say inappropriately
named, but poorly named

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:12.740
or unfortunately
named because we

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:15.198
have a product called Data Flow
that sits in the data flow.

00:20:15.198 --> 00:20:16.934
But try not to be confused.

00:20:16.934 --> 00:20:18.350
Bottom line, what
it's doing there

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.016
is it's running through a number
of different analytics systems,

00:20:21.016 --> 00:20:24.470
so BigQuery, Cloud ML, Datalab--

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:28.400
sorry, Vision Datalab
Cloud ML, and it's

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:30.850
analyzing the data, adding
insights to the data,

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.860
and then also saving it
into persistent storage.

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:37.310
At that point, there are
APIs that digest the data

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:40.440
and send that data
to different clients.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:45.050
You've got dashboards, so this
is the parking garage operator.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.190
They can see in realtime
what's going on.

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.010
Payments-- so if you're
making a payment,

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.570
keeping that data accurate.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:53.630
Signage, so you know how many
spots are available or free

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:54.920
in a particular area.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.540
Our favorite, enforcement here.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:59.900
Yes, they are helping
folks when you

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:02.537
have overstayed your welcome
in a particular parking spot.

00:21:02.537 --> 00:21:04.370
And then finally, there's
a metrics pipeline

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:06.350
for folks internal
in the company

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:08.600
to understand how the
network is performing

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.710
and perhaps even
troubleshoot some issues.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:14.000
So all of this is done without
operating any infrastructure.

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.710
And don't take my word for it.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:17.600
Listen to their CTO.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:19.649
In fact he's here at GCP Next.

00:21:19.649 --> 00:21:22.190
You'll recognize him from that
wonderful beard that he's got.

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:24.060
If you see him,
tell him I said, hi.

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:27.230
But just listen to the
efficiency and the productivity

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:29.870
that he has gotten out
of operating a Serverless

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:31.810
Even-driven infrastructure.

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:33.650
They were able to
completely re-architect

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.820
their platform, something that
took them 12 years to build,

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.650
in less than 18 months
using Serverless.

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:43.070
That allowed them to then
expand their business globally.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:45.230
Also Cloud Functions, our
Serverless Even-driven

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:48.320
framework, was the foundation
for their Smart Cloud data

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.840
intelligence platform, and
it provides their customers

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:53.780
with instant to-the-second
accurate data intelligence

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:57.180
and scale for millions
of transactions each day.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.050
So a very sophisticated, very
powerful system operating

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.840
absolutely no infrastructure.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.350
So let's switch
gears a little bit--

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:06.400
this is the last
customer example I have--

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.800
but to something that's
completely different,

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.910
to building a
mobile application.

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:14.410
So I want to tell you about
how development firm POSSE

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:21.052
used Serverless and Events
to build the Hamilton App.

00:22:21.052 --> 00:22:22.718
Let's hear a little
bit more about that.

00:22:22.718 --> 00:22:24.642
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:24.642 --> 00:22:26.850
SPEAKER 2: "Hamilton" is
