00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:27.650
So let's talk about the lottery.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.850
So for those of you who
aren't familiar, the lottery--

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:33.310
you know Hamilton is
extraordinarily popular--

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:36.940
the lottery is an opportunity to
not pay the entry price, which

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:38.204
I believe is around $200--

00:23:38.204 --> 00:23:40.120
but the reality is that
these things sell out,

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:42.850
so it's probably more
like $800 or $1,000--

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:45.340
but instead pay $10
to enter a lottery

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:46.870
and win a chance at tickets.

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:51.110
These are great tickets, I
think maybe 40 or so per show.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.180
You sign up for the lottery
two days beforehand.

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:55.570
A day beforehand,
the drawing happens,

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.410
and you're either
select as a winner,

00:23:57.410 --> 00:24:00.490
or you're told to enter the
lottery on a subsequent day.

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:02.300
And it's really interesting.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.910
I love making
developer's lives better,

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:06.910
and I love making
you more productive,

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:09.411
but I also love, and I hate
to use the trite line--

00:24:09.411 --> 00:24:10.910
in fact, I won't
use the trite line,

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:12.310
we're making the
world a better place--

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:14.230
but we're making the
world a better place,

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:17.290
in the sense that the creators
of Hamilton, with this lottery,

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:19.960
have allowed a whole new
demographic to actually access

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:21.250
Hamilton.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:22.810
And it's interesting
because they're

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.120
all about flipping
models on their head.

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:26.950
The whole play itself is about--

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:30.550
it's a majority minority cast
portraying the life and times

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.050
of Alexander Hamilton.

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:33.550
And this lottery,
as I said, kind of

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:37.670
does the same thing for the
demographics of the audiences.

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.490
So how they're doing that--

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:43.030
so a user says, I want to
be part of the lottery.

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:44.014
Enter me.

00:24:44.014 --> 00:24:46.180
That request then gets
written to Realtime Database,

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:49.980
which the backing store for
all of the Hamilton app.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:51.680
It then triggers
a Cloud function,

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:53.620
which first goes
through Firebase Auth

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.990
to say that this is
a legitimate request.

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:57.700
That function then
registers the fact

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.890
that the user has chosen to
enter the particular lottery.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:04.060
There's is a Cron job that
then operates down here

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:09.120
on a daily basis, triggering a
Cloud function that says who's

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:10.630
won on that particular day.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:12.350
It selects the
winners, writes it back

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:14.800
into the realtime
database, and also

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:17.510
notifies the lucky seat
that these people have won,

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.670
print their tickets,
and the users

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:23.770
are told of their winning
status or to enter

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.410
the lottery the next day.

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:27.680
Really interesting feature.

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:31.460
Next feature-- the
HamCam, the selfie app.

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:32.950
So this is a pretty
simple feature,

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.780
but again, it's
completely Serverless,

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:36.650
completely Even-driven.

00:25:36.650 --> 00:25:40.352
Users basically take a photo,
take a selfie in the app.

00:25:40.352 --> 00:25:42.060
It then writes directly
to Cloud Storage.

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.185
This is actually a lot like
our theoretical example

00:25:44.185 --> 00:25:46.990
that we went through,
mediated by Firebase Auth

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.510
just to make sure that this
is a legitimate request.

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:51.220
That then kicks off
a Cloud Function,

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:53.740
which crops and
resizes the image, then

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:56.650
writes that image URL back
to the realtime database

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:00.670
so that the user now sees their
wonderful selfie in the app.

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:02.860
And POSSE told us
that we made it

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:06.040
so easy for them with
the realtime database,

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:08.830
with Cloud Functions,
with Cloud Storage,

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.897
to build this feature
that, instead of operating

00:26:10.897 --> 00:26:13.480
infrastructure, they could spend
much more time on just making

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:15.521
it fun, making it interesting,
and adding effects

00:26:15.521 --> 00:26:16.990
like this to the app.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.560
And then finally, last example
I'll show you from "Hamilton"

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:25.140
is the merchandising,
just buying merchandise

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:26.230
in the store.

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:28.594
So the user says, I
want to buy something.

00:26:28.594 --> 00:26:30.510
That then kicks off two
different Cloud called

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:31.360
functions.

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:34.110
The first contact's
the Magento API,

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.110
which is an API for managing
e-commerce stores, things

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:42.690
like skew management pricing,
inventory management, all

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:44.350
done via Cloud Functions.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:48.420
The next function then contacts
Stripe to make payments.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:50.631
And this is a really
interesting application,

00:26:50.631 --> 00:26:52.505
kind of a perfect
application, of Serverless,

00:26:52.505 --> 00:26:56.400
in a sense, in that Stripe
doesn't trust mobile clients,

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:59.290
doesn't trust desktop
clients, to make transactions,

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:01.380
so it requires you
to have a private API

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:03.300
key that sits on a server.

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:04.980
So if you're a very
large site that

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.200
does tons and tons of
transactions per day,

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:08.760
maybe this is a non-issue.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.070
But if you're an app like
Hamilton, where most of it's

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.650
about consuming content
and not really about buying

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:16.170
merchandise, then having
that infrastructure,

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:18.440
operating that infrastructure,
is quite a burden.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:20.915
Cloud Functions allows
you to do that server key

00:27:20.915 --> 00:27:23.040
without operating any
infrastructure whatsoever, so

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.142
a perfect application
for Serverless.

00:27:27.924 --> 00:27:29.840
All right, so those are
just a couple examples

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:33.530
of what customers are doing
with Serverless, with Events,

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:35.510
to build very
sophisticated systems.

00:27:35.510 --> 00:27:37.610
And now, I want to
show you something

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:42.360
that we can build very simply
using Serverless and Events.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:45.920
So we're going to come back to
that example I showed earlier,

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:47.690
the Gallery app.

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:49.910
So we have a gallery.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:53.030
It is backed by Cloud Firestore
that just went beta today.

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:54.850
Hopefully you all saw that.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:59.374
It's an infinitely
scalable NoSQL database.

00:27:59.374 --> 00:28:01.790
And what we're going to do is
we're going to upload photo.

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:03.450
It's going to hit Twilio.

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:05.970
We're then going to save that
down to storage into profiles.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.120
We're then going to index
the data, basically take

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:10.850
the name that was entered
and the image URL,

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:13.454
put that into Cloud Firestore,
which will update our gallery.

00:28:13.454 --> 00:28:15.620
And then that'll kick off
another function that then

00:28:15.620 --> 00:28:17.870
analyzes the image,
pulls it out,

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:22.760
and extracts the visual
information via the Vision API,

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:25.190
writes it back into
Cloud Firestore-- sorry,

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:26.370
my pointer is dying--

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:28.120
and then updates the
gallery in real time.

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:42.070
And so with that, let's
switch to the demo, please.

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:44.500
All right so here's
our Gallery app.

